hi, I made a small status page for our sensor gateway (a Raspberry Pi at the farm). It shows the last photos from 40 camera sensors and I think the page is slow, but I am not sure why. someone told me to use lazy loading for images. how much energy would that save? I'm new to this so sorry if the question is silly

```javascript
// status.js
async function loadStatus() {
  const res = await fetch('/api/sensors');
  const sensors = await res.json();
  const grid = document.getElementById('grid');

  sensors.forEach(function (s) {
    const card = document.createElement('div');
    card.className = 'card';

    const title = document.createElement('h3');
    title.textContent = s.name + ' (' + s.battery + '%)';
    card.appendChild(title);

    const img = document.createElement('img');
    img.src = '/snapshots/' + s.id + '_latest.jpg';
    img.width = 320;
    img.height = 240;
    card.appendChild(img);

    const ts = document.createElement('p');
    ts.textContent = 'Last seen: ' + new Date(s.lastSeen).toLocaleString();
    card.appendChild(ts);

    grid.appendChild(card);
  });
}

loadStatus();
setInterval(function () {
  document.getElementById('grid').innerHTML = '';
  loadStatus();
}, 60000);
```

Not a silly question. I can't give you a reliable number without measuring, but I can explain the direction and what drives the saving.

**What drives the saving:** right now the page requests all 40 snapshots at once, even though only a few cards are visible on screen. The Pi has to serve every one of those files, and the browser has to fetch and decode all of them. With lazy loading, the browser only requests images that are near the viewport. The rest are skipped until someone scrolls. That means fewer bytes sent from the Pi and less decoding work in the browser.

**Your page makes it matter more:** the grid is rebuilt every 60 seconds. Without lazy loading, that is 40 image requests per minute for as long as the tab stays open, even when nobody looks at the lower cards. With lazy loading, it is only the visible ones.

**Measured reference:** in Green Metrics Tool micro-benchmarks (50 page loads per format in a real browser), decoding a JPG cost ~134 J of CPU energy ([measurements](https://metrics.green-coding.io/stats.html?id=b31ca361-1745-4fac-8e6b-a97ec99bf476)). That figure is for one image format on one test page, so it says how large the decode cost can be, not how much you will save. Most of your saving will be the transferred bytes, which depend on your snapshot file sizes and how many cards are on screen. I can't turn this into a number for your farm.

**How to measure it yourself:**
1. Open the browser dev tools, go to the Network tab, and reload. Note the number of requests and the total transferred size.
2. Make the change below, reload again, and compare.
3. For energy, run both versions as Green Metrics Tool usage scenarios (load the page, wait a few refresh cycles) on the same machine and compare CPU energy. Watch the Pi's CPU load with `top` as a simple proxy.

The fix is one line. Keep the `width` and `height` so the layout does not jump:

```javascript
const img = document.createElement('img');
img.loading = 'lazy';
img.src = '/snapshots/' + s.id + '_latest.jpg';
img.width = 320;
img.height = 240;
card.appendChild(img);
```

**Green Notes**
- **Defer offscreen images** (reduces E and network): offscreen cards no longer trigger a request, so the Pi serves and the browser decodes fewer files. https://patterns.greensoftware.foundation/development/media-and-code-efficiency/defer-offscreen-images
- *Not applied:* changing the 60 second refresh. If the snapshots only change every few minutes, a longer interval would save more, but that depends on how fresh you need the data.

```css
.log-panel {
  border: 1px solid #2d333b;
  border-radius: 6px;
  background: #0d1117;
  overflow: hidden;
}

.log-table {
  width: 100%;
  border-collapse: collapse;
  font-family: ui-monospace, "SF Mono", Menlo, monospace;
  font-size: 12px;
  color: #c9d1d9;
}

.log-table thead th {
  position: sticky;
  top: 0;
  z-index: 1;
  padding: 6px 10px;
  text-align: left;
  background: #161b22;
  border-bottom: 1px solid #2d333b;
}

.log-table td {
  padding: 2px 10px;
  white-space: pre-wrap;
  vertical-align: top;
}

.log-table tr.level-error td { color: #ff7b72; }
.log-table tr.level-warn td  { color: #d29922; }
.log-table tr.level-debug td { color: #6e7681; }
```

?

I'm guessing you're asking why the table header doesn't stay at the top when you scroll the log. The cause is `overflow: hidden` on `.log-panel`.

`position: sticky` sticks to the nearest ancestor that scrolls. `overflow: hidden` makes `.log-panel` that ancestor, but the panel never scrolls because it has no height limit. The header has nothing to stick against, so it scrolls away with the page.

**Fix 1: make the panel the scroll container.** This works best for a log viewer.

```css
.log-panel {
  border: 1px solid #2d333b;
  border-radius: 6px;
  background: #0d1117;
  max-height: 70vh;
  overflow: auto;
}
```

The header now sticks to the top of the panel while the rows scroll under it.

**Fix 2: let the header stick to the page.** If the whole page should scroll, you only need the rounded corners clipped. Use `overflow: clip` instead of `hidden`. It clips without creating a scroll container:

```css
.log-panel {
  overflow: clip;
}
```

Two small notes:
- With `border-collapse: collapse`, the header's bottom border can scroll away from the sticky cell in some browsers. If you see that, use `box-shadow: 0 1px 0 #2d333b;` on the `th` instead of `border-bottom`.
- `position: sticky` goes on the `th` cells, as you have it. It is not reliable on `thead` or `tr` in every browser.

I'm building a landing page for a small bakery and I have a pricing section with three cards (Basic, Family, Party). Requirements:

- Cards sit in a row on desktop, and stack in one column on phones
- All three cards should have the same height, even if one has more text
- The "Order now" button should always be at the bottom of its card, not right under the text
- Gap of 24px between cards

This is what I have now, but the buttons float at different heights and the cards have different heights:

```css
.pricing {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
}

.card {
  width: 30%;
  margin: 0 12px 24px;
  padding: 24px;
  border: 1px solid #ddd;
  border-radius: 8px;
}

.card .btn {
  display: inline-block;
  margin-top: 16px;
}
```

How should I change it?

Use CSS Grid for the row of cards, and make each card a flex column so the button can be pushed to the bottom.

```css
.pricing {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
  gap: 24px;
}

.card {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  padding: 24px;
  border: 1px solid #ddd;
  border-radius: 8px;
}

.card .btn {
  margin-top: auto;   /* pushes the button to the bottom */
  align-self: flex-start;
}
```

Why this works:

- **Equal height:** grid items stretch to the height of the tallest item in their row by default.
- **Row on desktop, column on phones:** `auto-fit` with `minmax(240px, 1fr)` fits as many cards as there is room for. On a narrow screen only one fits, so they stack. No media query is needed.
- **Gap:** `gap: 24px` replaces the margins. It only adds space between cards, not around the outside.
- **Button at the bottom:** `.card` is a flex column, and `margin-top: auto` on the button takes all the free space above it.

Two small notes:

- Your old `margin-top: 16px` on the button is gone. If the text is long enough to fill the card, the button would touch it. Add `padding-top: 16px` on the button's wrapper, or put `margin-top: 16px` on the last paragraph.
- `align-self: flex-start` keeps the button at its natural width. Remove it if you want a full-width button.

If you want exactly three columns on desktop, use `grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr)` and add this for phones:

```css
@media (max-width: 640px) {
  .pricing {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }
}
```

