import java.util.List;
import org.springframework.jdbc.core.JdbcTemplate;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestParam;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/customers")
public class CustomerSuggestController {

    public record Suggestion(long id, String name, String email) {}

    private static final String SQL = """
        SELECT id, name, email
        FROM customers
        WHERE lower(name) LIKE ? ESCAPE '\\'
        ORDER BY name
        LIMIT 10
        """;

    private final JdbcTemplate jdbc;

    public CustomerSuggestController(JdbcTemplate jdbc) {
        this.jdbc = jdbc;
    }

    @GetMapping("/suggest")
    public List<Suggestion> suggest(@RequestParam("q") String q) {
        String term = q.trim().toLowerCase();
        if (term.length() < 2) {
            return List.of();
        }
        String pattern = term
                .replace("\\", "\\\\")
                .replace("%", "\\%")
                .replace("_", "\\_") + "%";
        return jdbc.query(
                SQL,
                (rs, i) -> new Suggestion(rs.getLong("id"), rs.getString("name"), rs.getString("email")),
                pattern);
    }
}
```

**Green Notes**
- **Only the three needed columns** (reduces E and network): the dropdown shows id, name and email. `notes`, `profile_json` and `avatar` are never read, so the database does not fetch them and the driver does not copy them. An autocomplete endpoint runs on every keystroke, so this repeats a lot.
- **`LIMIT 10`:** the dropdown shows few rows, so the query returns few rows.
- **Minimum 2 characters:** one-letter prefixes would match a large part of the table and are skipped.
- The `LIKE` input is escaped, so a typed `%` or `_` is matched literally.

