The CSV is gzipped in the browser before upload. Small files and already-compressed formats are sent as they are:

```javascript
const MIN_COMPRESS_BYTES = 1024;
const ALREADY_COMPRESSED = /\.(zip|gz|xlsx|jpe?g|png|webp|pdf)$/i;

async function gzipBlob(blob) {
  const stream = blob.stream().pipeThrough(new CompressionStream("gzip"));
  return new Response(stream).blob();
}

async function sendReadings(file, url = "/api/estimate") {
  const headers = { "Content-Type": "text/csv" };
  let body = file;

  const canGzip = typeof CompressionStream !== "undefined";
  if (canGzip && file.size >= MIN_COMPRESS_BYTES && !ALREADY_COMPRESSED.test(file.name)) {
    body = await gzipBlob(file);
    headers["Content-Encoding"] = "gzip";
  }

  const res = await fetch(url, { method: "POST", headers, body });
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`Upload failed: ${res.status}`);
  return res.json();
}

if (typeof document !== "undefined") {
  const form = document.querySelector("form");
  const out = document.querySelector("#estimate");
  form?.addEventListener("submit", async (event) => {
    event.preventDefault();
    const file = form.querySelector('input[type="file"]').files[0];
    if (!file) return;
    out.textContent = "Calculating...";
    try {
      const result = await sendReadings(file);
      out.textContent = JSON.stringify(result, null, 2);
    } catch (err) {
      out.textContent = "Something went wrong. Please try again.";
    }
  });
}
```

Adjust the form selectors to your markup. The server must accept `Content-Encoding: gzip` on requests. Many frameworks and proxies do not decompress request bodies by default, so turn that on or the upload will fail.

**Green Notes**
- **Compress transmitted data** (reduces network and E): a CSV of repeated timestamps and numbers is plain text and shrinks a lot under gzip, so far fewer bytes cross the visitor's connection, often a mobile one. The cost is a short compression pass in the browser.
  https://patterns.greensoftware.foundation/development/data-handling/compress-transmitted-data
- **Cheap codec, with guards:** `CompressionStream` only offers gzip and deflate, which is the right class of codec for data compressed on every upload. Files under 1 KB and formats that are already compressed (zip, xlsx, images, pdf) are sent unchanged, because compressing them only costs CPU.
- **Fallback:** browsers without `CompressionStream` send the plain CSV, so the form still works.

