```java
public class WaitlistImporter {
    private static final String INSERT_SQL =
            "INSERT INTO waitlist (email, referrer_id) VALUES (?, ?)";
    private static final int BATCH_SIZE = 1000;

    private final DataSource ds;

    public WaitlistImporter(DataSource ds) {
        this.ds = ds;
    }

    // lines come from the marketing export for the launch landing page
    public int importLines(List<String> lines) throws SQLException {
        int saved = 0;
        try (Connection c = ds.getConnection();
             PreparedStatement ps = c.prepareStatement(INSERT_SQL)) {
            c.setAutoCommit(false);
            try {
                int pending = 0;
                for (String line : lines) {
                    String[] p = line.split(",");
                    if (p.length < 2) continue;
                    ps.setString(1, p[0].trim().toLowerCase());
                    ps.setInt(2, parseReferrer(p[1].trim()));
                    ps.addBatch();
                    if (++pending == BATCH_SIZE) {
                        ps.executeBatch();
                        c.commit();
                        saved += pending;
                        pending = 0;
                    }
                }
                if (pending > 0) {
                    ps.executeBatch();
                    c.commit();
                    saved += pending;
                }
            } catch (SQLException e) {
                c.rollback();
                throw e;
            }
        }
        return saved;
    }

    // Same result as Integer.parseInt(s), or 0 if it is not a number.
    // Checks the text first, so a normal "-" or "n/a" does not throw.
    private static int parseReferrer(String s) {
        int start = (!s.isEmpty() && (s.charAt(0) == '-' || s.charAt(0) == '+')) ? 1 : 0;
        if (s.length() == start) return 0;
        for (int i = start; i < s.length(); i++) {
            if (Character.digit(s.charAt(i), 10) < 0) return 0;
        }
        try {
            return Integer.parseInt(s);
        } catch (NumberFormatException e) { // only for digits that overflow int
            return 0;
        }
    }
}
```

**Green Notes**
- **One connection, batched inserts** (reduces E and network): the old code opened a connection and sent one statement per line. Now it sends one statement per 1,000 rows over a single connection. For a big export this removes thousands of round trips and connection setups. With the PostgreSQL JDBC driver you can add `reWriteBatchedInserts=true` to the URL so each batch becomes a multi-row insert.
- **No exception for expected input** (reduces E): placeholders like `-` or `n/a` in the referrer column are normal, but each one used to throw a `NumberFormatException` and build a stack trace. The helper checks the text first. `try/catch` stays only for the rare overflow case, so results are the same as before.
- **Behavior change:** the old version committed every row on its own. Now each chunk of 1,000 is one transaction. If a row fails, that chunk is rolled back and the exception is thrown. Earlier chunks stay committed.
- For a list of a few dozen lines the gain from batching would be small. It pays off because this runs on large exports.

